          <img src="/images/pexels-hanfu-woman.jpg" srcset="/images/opt/pexels-hanfu-woman-400w.webp 400w, /images/opt/pexels-hanfu-woman-600w.webp 600w" sizes="(max-width: 768px) 100vw, 420px" alt="Woman in serene traditional Hanfu portrait in soft daylight" width="1400" height="933" loading="lazy">
        </div>
        <div class="media-card-body" style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;justify-content:center;">
          <p style="font-size:0.8125rem;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:0.08em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent);margin-bottom:0.5rem;">The Look</p>
          <h3 style="font-size:1.375rem;">Muted colors, narrow sleeves, quiet elegance.</h3>
          <p style="font-size:0.9375rem;color:var(--color-muted);margin-top:0.75rem;">Song Hanfu trades drama for refinement. Think pale greens, blues, and whites, slim vertical lines, and fabric that whispers rather than billows.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2>What Defines the Song Hanfu Look</h2>
      <ul style="list-style:disc;padding-left:1.5rem;color:var(--color-muted);font-size:0.9375rem;line-height:1.8;">
        <li><strong>Slim, vertical silhouette:</strong> Unlike the voluminous Tang, Song Hanfu creates a long, lean line from shoulder to ankle.</li>
        <li><strong>The beizi jacket:</strong> An open-front, long vest-like jacket with narrow sleeves that defines the entire Song aesthetic.</li>
        <li><strong>Muted color palette:</strong> Celadon green, powder blue, moon white, dusty rose, lavender, ink black, and grey.</li>
        <li><strong>Delicate, sparse embroidery:</strong> A single plum blossom sprig or bamboo leaf, not the dense patterns of Tang and Ming.</li>
        <li><strong>Lightweight fabrics:</strong> Song weavers perfected sheer silk gauze and fine linen that drapes like water.</li>
        <li><strong>Natural elegance:</strong> The overall effect is intellectual, refined, and quietly confident -- scholar aesthetic at its peak.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 style="margin-top:2rem;">Who Is Song Hanfu Best For?</h2>
      <p class="text-muted">Song Hanfu is ideal for those who value subtlety over spectacle:</p>
      <ul style="list-style:disc;padding-left:1.5rem;color:var(--color-muted);font-size:0.9375rem;line-height:1.8;">
        <li><strong>Everyday wear:</strong> The beizi jacket works with modern clothing -- wear it over a simple dress for a contemporary look with Song DNA.</li>
        <li><strong>Tea ceremonies and quiet gatherings:</strong> The Song aesthetic was made for contemplative, cultured occasions.</li>
        <li><strong>Petite frames:</strong> The slim silhouette does not overwhelm smaller body types the way Tang wide sleeves can.</li>
        <li><strong>Beginners seeking subtlety:</strong> If you want to wear Hanfu without feeling costumed, Song is often the right answer.</li>
        <li><strong>Hot weather:</strong> Song's lightweight fabrics and minimal layers make it the most comfortable Hanfu for summer.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 style="margin-top:2rem;">Key Song Hanfu Garments You Need</h2>
      <div class="grid-2" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:1rem;">
        <div class="vs-card">
          <h3>Beizi Jacket</h3>
          <p>The essential Song piece. A long open-front jacket with narrow sleeves. Buy the beizi first -- it transforms any outfit into Song Hanfu.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="vs-card">
          <h3>Song Ruqun Set</h3>
          <p>A slim-fitting top and skirt worn beneath the beizi. The skirt sits at the natural waist with a narrower, more column-like shape than Tang skirts.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="vs-card">
          <h3>Folding Fan</h3>
          <p>The Song scholar's accessory: a paper or silk folding fan, ideally with calligraphy or a simple landscape painting.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="vs-card">
          <h3>Simple Hairpin</h3>
          <p>A single carved sandalwood or silver hairpin in a sleek bun. Minimal, intentional, elegant.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

